  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 306 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: This afternoon into tonight will be one of the more \nactive periods this week as a low pressure system to our north drags \na series of fronts through our forecast area. The day will start off \ndry, but rain will move in from the west during the afternoon hours \nas a warm front approaches. Increasing southerly flow ahead of the \nsystem will result in developing gusty southerly winds today, \nespecially over Lake Champlain where gusts will reach 20 to 30+\nknots by this afternoon into this evening and a Lake Wind\nAdvisory is in effect. Outside of Lake Champlain, highest gusts\nthis afternoon will mainly be observed over the northern \nChamplain Valley, higher elevations of the Greens and \nAdirondacks, and over the northern slopes of the Adirondacks. \nGusts will be strongest ahead of the steady rain, then will \ndiminish as rain becomes widespread due to low- level \nstabilization. \n\nThe increased southerly flow will bring PWats to 1.5 to 2.0 inches \nby this evening, which is around 150 to 200% of climatological \nnormal for our area for this time of year. This anomalously \nmoist air mass will support some periods of moderate to heavy \nrain this evening into tonight. Models are indicating rates will\nmainly remain under a tenth of an inch an hour, but some \nheavier showers and thunderstorms could produce brief rainfall \nrates up to a quarter to a half inch an hour. Overall, the flood\nrisk this evening into tonight is low (WPC's Excessive Rainfall\nOutlook has our entire area in a marginal risk of rainfall \nexceeding flash flood guidance). However, if any heavier storms \ntrack over the same area, those areas will need to be watched \nclosely. \n\nIn addition to moderate to briefly heavy rainfall, some (mainly \nelevated) thunderstorms are anticipated this evening into early \ntonight. The surface based instability and overall severe threat \nwill be limited by a maintained low level inversion over most areas. \nHowever, the environment is slightly more favorable locally over the \nsouthern Saint Lawrence Valley, where the development of some \nmarginal SBCAPE is progged.  The amount of surface based instability \nis still somewhat in question, but latest SPC Convective Outlook \nmaintains the Marginal Risk for most of Saint Lawrence County, with \nthe Slight Risk staying just to our southwest. The main threat from \nthunderstorms in northern New York will be gusty winds and locally \nheavy rainfall. The remainder of our forecast area remains within \nthe General Thunder contour, and risk of severe weather outside of \nthe Saint Lawrence Valley is low tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2:  Widespread rainfall will come to an end by Thursday \nmorning, giving way to generally drier weather with afternoon \nshowers possible each day through Saturday.  A slow moving upper-\nlevel low will linger to our north/northeast through Saturday, \nsupporting seasonably cool conditions with periods of instability-\ndriven afternoon showers.  Coverage of showers will be greatest over \nhigher terrain. Meanwhile, the Champlain Valley and Saint Lawrence \nValley will remain mainly dry during this timeframe. By Sunday,\nany remaining shower activity will end as ridging builds in \nfrom the west. Subsidence will allow for more sunshine and a \npleasant, albeit seasonably cool, day.",
